    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d566">

                <pb facs="0681" n="669" sameAs="frus:frus1942v01/pg669"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">845.01/198: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Bombay</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Donovan</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Bombay</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1942-06-08T15:00:00+05:30">June 8, 1942—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received June 8—2:26 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>424. This office had no correspondence whatever either direct or indirect with
                    the Maharaja of Indore on this subject. Your 216.<anchor n="85" xml:id="d566-f1"
                    /> Neither I nor any member of my staff were associated in any way with the
                    preparation of this letter.</p>
                <p>At the request of a friend who is a press correspondent, I delivered to Reuters a
                    sealed cover which unknown to me contained text of Maharaja’s letter. I did this
                    as a personal favor to save time for my friend since I happened to be passing by
                    Reuters office. Later that day I was informed by my friend of contents of cover.
                    See my airmail despatch 567, June 3.<anchor n="86" xml:id="d566-f2"/> This
                    despatch and my 400<anchor n="87" xml:id="d566-f3"/> were written after full
                    text of letter had appeared Bombay <hi rendition="#italic">Times of India</hi>
                    May 29.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Donovan</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
